WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.960
Your worktop takes every hit, spill and spark in the room.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.312
So why do most people choose it last?

00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:13.788
The wrong surface fails fast: chemicals stain laminate, sparks scorch it,

00:00:13.788 --> 00:00:15.859
and static damages electronics.

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:18.828
Then the whole bench looks and works older than it is.

00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:22.912
And replacing a worktop later means stopping work at that station.

00:00:23.512 --> 00:00:25.272
Choose by what touches the top.

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:32.018
PLW, postform laminate, suits general assembly and packing, and is the most economical.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:36.730
PELW, electrostatic laminate, protects electronics.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:42.208
HPL, high-pressure laminate, resists solvents and mild acids in labs.

00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:45.732
Stainless steel handles heat, corrosion and hygiene.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:50.093
GI-clad tops take impact, oil, sparks and grinding.

00:00:50.373 --> 00:00:54.327
Think about the worst thing that will ever touch your top, not the average day.

00:00:54.927 --> 00:01:00.189
Furnisys offers these worktops across its workbenches, workstations and cabinets,

00:01:00.189 --> 00:01:02.853
and a rubber mat can protect any of them.

00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:06.457
Match the surface to the work, not the catalogue photo.

00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:08.245
What touches your bench most?

00:01:08.525 --> 00:01:13.492
Comment below, and next, see when HPL is enough and when you need stainless steel.

00:01:14.092 --> 00:01:18.337
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

